Also measured: the three <label>s at :98,:121,:145 have {htmlFor: null, hasControlChild: false}, and clicking the visible 'Color space' text changes the listbox count 0 -> 0 (p2.mjs).
- **Mechanism:** Because the labels are orphans they surface as bare text nodes, and each trigger's aria-label then repeats the identical string — linear AT traversal reads 'Color space … Color space, combobox, OKLab.' Prior rounds established the orphaning and the 'Size mismatch' / 'Size mismatch strategy' drift; the duplication of the SAME string is the third consequence and the one a user hears. Separately MixConfigBar.vue:168 is `<Blend class="w-4 h-4" />` with no aria-hidden, so a decorative glyph enters the a11y tree as an unnamed img inside a named button — its own sibling does it right (MixSourceSelector.vue:173 `<Plus … aria-hidden="true" />`). This is NOT the namelessButtons:1 that REPORT.json records for /#/mix, which r2 correctly attributed to button.send-btn in an unrelated widget.
- **Reproduction:** node scratchpad/wbmix/p5.mjs (ariaSnapshot) and p2.mjs (label-click listbox count before/after).
- **Proposed cure:** One decision applied at the recipe: the visible text IS the accessible name — `<label :for="id">` on a real id, and delete the three hand-written aria-label strings. That restores the click target, kills the duplicate announcement, and makes the caption's legibility a correctness property rather than a styling preference. Add aria-hidden="true" to the Blend glyph.
